HST Office: a Vietnamese .docx saved by Word opened up to 8 times slower than the same document
saved by HST Writer. Word stores runs with the same properties side by side, most visibly one run
per precomposed Vietnamese letter (U+1E00-U+1EFF) when the East Asian font differs from the Latin
one: a 300-citation thesis has 204,224 runs instead of 4,146. The import inserted each run with
its own SwXText::appendTextPortion() call, which builds a text cursor, a text range with a UNO
bookmark and an undo group per run: about 25 µs per run.

DomainMapper now keeps the text of a plain run (no open field, no redline, no comment, no special
character) and appends to it the text of the following runs that go to the same text with the same
properties; one appendTextPortion() call inserts it. Every other stream event (properties,
bookmarks, fields, shapes, paragraph and section ends, substreams ...) and every other kind of
portion first inserts the kept text, so the document is complete whenever anything else reads or
changes it.
Runs with their own run properties are still inserted one by one.

Measured on the batch4 build (fastest of 7 loads): the Word-saved 300-citation thesis 4.97 s -> 1.45 s,
a Word-saved 400-paragraph Vietnamese text 1.15 s -> 0.52 s. 2,117 .docx and .rtf files of the
upstream test corpus (sw/qa) convert to the same ODF (only time stamps and generated IDs differ).

Test: tests/runtime/check-docx-word-interop.py (split runs: text, bold run, bookmark position).
